  "reconstruction": {
    "text": "There exists a single family (mu_n) indexed by all natural numbers n including zero, with each mu_n a genuine Borel probability measure on the real line, having the following three properties simultaneously. For every natural n, let C_n be the finite carrier of all Boolean functions on the indices {0,...,n-1}, let N_n = |C_n| (hence N_n = 2^n), and let U_n = (N_n)^(-1) times counting measure on C_n, where the cardinal and its inverse are taken in the extended nonnegative reals. Define s(true)=1 and s(false)=-1 in the reals and T_n(epsilon) = (sqrt(n))^(-1) times the sum of s(epsilon(j)) over all j<n. The underlying measure of mu_n is exactly the measurable pushforward (T_n)_* U_n. In addition, the underlying measure of mu_0 is the Dirac probability measure at real zero. Finally, as n tends to infinity through the natural numbers, mu_(n+1) converges to the real Gaussian probability measure with mean zero and variance one in the canonical weak topology on real Borel probability measures. Equivalently, for every bounded continuous real test function f, the integrals of f against mu_(n+1) converge to its integral against that Gaussian. The formula defining T_n uses the totalized real inverse, so inverse zero is zero and the empty coordinate sum at n=0 is zero; C_0 consists of its unique empty Boolean configuration and its counting/cardinality law has mass one. The theorem has no caller-supplied premises: the probability-law family, its pushforward identities, its zero-index identity, and its successor-indexed weak convergence are all asserted outputs.",
